<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Tartu (Russian–Estonian)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1920">1920</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonian-war-of-independence">Estonian War of Independence</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Tartu was signed on 2 February 1920 between the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> and Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonian-war-of-independence">Estonian War of Independence</a>. Russia recognised Estonia's independence in perpetuity and agreed a border that gave Estonia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivangorod">Ivangorod</a> and Petseri. It was the first international recognition of Estonia, which regards the treaty as a foundation of its statehood.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tartu-russian-estonian.jpg" alt="Treaty of Tartu (Russian–Estonian)" width="320" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Armin Lomp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joffe_signing_the_Treaty_of_Tartu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> had been a province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Imperial Russia</a> since 1721. In 1917, three years into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, the Russian Empire fell into revolution and civil war. As part of this larger conflict the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a> declared independence from the warring Russian and German empires, and they won their freedom in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonian-war-of-independence">Estonian War of Independence</a> of 1918 to 1920.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The treaty established the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, affirmed the right of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a> to return to Estonia and of Russian people to return to Russia, and required that Estonian movable property evacuated to Russia in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> be returned. Russia also agreed to absolve Estonia of Russian Imperial debt and to pay Estonia 15 million gold rubles, a share of the gold reserves of the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> further agreed to grant concessions to exploit one million hectares of Russian forest land and to build a railway line from the Estonian border to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. Jaan Poska signed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> and Adolf Joffe for Soviet Russia, together with other representatives of both parties. Ratifications were exchanged in Moscow on 30 March 1920, and the treaty was registered in the League of Nations Treaty Series on 12 July 1922.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The treaty has been regarded as the birth certificate of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> because it was the first de jure recognition of the state. It was also of great importance to diplomatically isolated Soviet Russia, and Lenin expressed satisfaction with it as &quot;an incomparable victory over Western imperialism&quot;. Some members of the Entente opposed the treaty, intending to keep Soviet Russia in international isolation.</p><p>After the signing, Soviet Russia did not fulfil several points of the treaty. The migration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a> was obstructed, and the museum collections of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-tartu">University of Tartu</a> were not returned from Voronezh. Estonia was occupied by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1940 to 1941 and again from 1944 to 1991.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Tartu (Estonia–Russia)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Treaty of Tartu</b> in 1920 recognised the independence of the Republic of Estonia.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-the-working-people-of-estonia">Commune of the Working People of Estonia</a></li></ul></section>
